<wpf:ResourceDictionary xml:space="preserve" xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml" xmlns:s="clr-namespace:System;assembly=mscorlib" xmlns:ss="urn:shemas-jetbrains-com:settings-storage-xaml" xmlns:wpf="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation">
	<!-- The repo's first per-project settings layer, and the reason it exists is scope.

	     MethodHasAsyncOverload sits at WARNING in the solution file, where it guards the async
	     pipelines in Core, Roslyn, Xunit and the CLI: a sync call that blocks one of those is a
	     real defect. It cannot mean that here. Every finding it raises in this project is
	     File.ReadAllText or File.WriteAllText in a test's arrange or assert block — fixture setup
	     on tiny files, strictly sequential, inside an async Task test method whose async exists to
	     await the runner under test rather than to overlap anything. Awaiting them yields no
	     thread, saves no blocking, and puts await noise through arrange blocks that read as data.

	     Off here rather than demoted in the solution file, because demoting it there would give up
	     the guard everywhere. jb mounts project layers by default — the switch that suppresses
	     them names ProjectShared among the layers it turns off, and is not spelled literally in
	     this comment for the reason below — so the explicit WARNING beside them still reaches
	     every other project.

	     Three traps, each of which cost a probe here before this file worked:

	     1. XML forbids adjacent hyphens inside a comment. Spelling a jb switch literally makes
	        this file unparseable, and jb then ignores the whole layer SILENTLY: no error, no
	        warning, findings simply keep their solution-level severity. It reads exactly like the
	        mechanism not working.
	     2. A dot inside a rule ID escapes as _002E in these keys. This rule has none; one that
	        does needs the escape, and a literal dot produces a key ReSharper never reads.
	     3. Naming the solution settings file explicitly on the command line DEFEATS this layer.
	        jb mounts an explicitly-named settings file as a custom layer that sits above the
	        project layers, so the scope below is ignored and the solution-level WARNING applies
	        here after all. jb finds this file on its own; the run that proves the baseline is the
	        one that passes no settings path at all. Measured: 0 findings without, 83 with.

	     DotSettingsCleanupProfileTests pins this file present, well-formed, and DO_NOT_SHOW. -->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=MethodHasAsyncOverload/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>
</wpf:ResourceDictionary>
